"},{"id":"g1","kind":"general","question":"Several options may deliberately share one storage name (a "feature switch"). What rule decides which option's value ends up in `ctx.params[name]`, and what breaks the tie when two of them both fall back to their defaults?","key":"`Parameter.handle_parse_result` arbitrates each slot. It snapshots the existing value, the existing `ParameterSource` and whether the previous writer had an explicit default, then writes only if: the slot is still `UNSET`, or the new source is strictly more explicit (`source < existing_source` — `ParameterSource` is an IntEnum ordered PROMPT < COMMANDLINE < ENVIRONMENT < DEFAULT_MAP < DEFAULT), or the sources are equal and it is not the case that both are `DEFAULT` while the existing writer passed `default=` explicitly and the new one did not (`auto_would_downgrade_explicit`). A loser restores the winner's parameter source instead of overwriting it. The explicitness tie-break uses `Parameter._default_explicit`, which is `default is not UNSET` captured at construction time, so the option that was declared with `default=True` wins over sibling flags that got an auto-derived default.","lines":["src/click/core.py:2892-2920","src/click/core.py:195-231","src/click/core.py:2404-2410"],"check":"import click
