    click.echo(transformation)

r = CliRunner()
assert r.invoke(cli, []).output == 'upper\n'
assert r.invoke(cli, ['--lower']).output == 'lower\n'
assert r.invoke(cli, ['--upper']).output == 'upper\n'
"},{"id":"g2","kind":"general","question":"Click uses an internal `UNSET` sentinel to mean "no value at all". What guarantee does the library make about users ever seeing that sentinel, and where is it enforced?","key":"`UNSET` is an implementation detail that must never reach user code. It is converted to `None` at every boundary: `Command.parse_args` sweeps `ctx.params` after all parameters are handled and replaces every remaining `UNSET` with `None`; `Parameter.process_value` passes `None` (not `UNSET`) to a parameter callback and temporarily rewrites other `UNSET` entries of `ctx.params` to `None` while the callback runs (restoring them afterwards); `Context.invoke` turns an `UNSET` default into `None` before type casting; `Parameter._hide_unset` maps it to `None` for `to_info_dict` and for prompt defaults. The sweep is deliberately deferred until after parsing so that `UNSET` can still act as "missing" while several parameters compete for the same name.","lines":["src/click/core.py:1402-1413","src/click/core.py:2708-2772","src/click/core.py:915-927","src/click/core.py:2440-2446"],"check":"import click
