"},{"id":"g4","kind":"general","question":"`CliRunner` has two capture strategies. What does each capture, what are the restrictions, and what is the observable difference for code that calls `sys.stdout.fileno()`?","key":"`capture='sys'` (the default) only redirects the Python-level `sys.stdout`/`sys.stderr` to `_NamedTextIOWrapper`s over `BytesIO` buffers; `_original_fd` stays `-1`, so `fileno()` falls through to `TextIOWrapper` and raises `io.UnsupportedOperation` — which also means user code cannot `os.dup2` over the host test runner's real stdout. `capture='fd'` additionally starts `_FDCapture` on file descriptors 1 and 2, dup2-ing them to a temporary file so output from stale stream references, C extensions and subprocesses is captured too; in that mode `sys.stdout._original_fd` is set to the saved original fd so `fileno()` keeps working for consumers like faulthandler, and the captured bytes are merged into the stdout/stderr buffers in the `finally` block. `capture='fd'` raises `ValueError` on Windows, and any other value raises `ValueError` in `__init__`. If starting the fd capture raises `OSError`, it silently degrades to no fd capture.","lines":["src/click/testing.py:317-381","src/click/testing.py:653-672","src/click/testing.py:711-728","src/click/testing.py:156-200"],"check":"import io
