"},{"id":"g5","kind":"general","question":"Describe `Command.main`'s error policy: which exceptions it handles in standalone mode, the exit code each produces, and how behaviour changes with `standalone_mode=False`.","key":"In standalone mode `main` catches: `Exit` (uses `e.exit_code`, no message), `Abort` (prints "Aborted!" to stderr, exit code 1), `EOFError`/`KeyboardInterrupt` (echoes a blank line to stderr, then reports "Aborted!" with exit code 1), `ClickException` (calls `e.show()` and uses `e.exit_code` — 1 for `ClickException`, 2 for `UsageError` and its subclasses), and `OSError` with `errno.EPIPE` (wraps stdout/stderr in `_PacifyFlushWrapper` and exits 1; other OSErrors are re-raised). Each handler only records a `report` callable and an exit code; the teardown writes the message then calls `sys.exit(exit_code)`, and an outer handler catches a late `EOFError`/`KeyboardInterrupt` so the message may be lost but the intended exit code still wins. With `standalone_mode=False`, `Exit` returns its code, `Abort`/`ClickException` propagate to the caller, `EOFError`/`KeyboardInterrupt` are converted to `Abort`, and the return value of `invoke` is returned instead of exiting.","lines":["src/click/core.py:1576-1645","src/click/exceptions.py:35-77"],"check":"import click
