"},{"id":"g7","kind":"general","question":"Why can reading `Option.default` directly mislead you, and what does `Option.get_default` do differently?","key":"`Option.__init__` deliberately keeps the raw constructor value, so `default` stays the `UNSET` sentinel when the user passed nothing (that is what `_default_explicit`/feature-switch arbitration relies on); the only eager coercion is `default = 0` for `count` options. The auto-derived defaults are applied lazily by `_resolve_lazy_default`, called from `get_default` and from `to_info_dict`: `UNSET` becomes `()` for a non-required, non-prompted multiple flag and `False` for a non-required boolean flag, and a literal `True` becomes `flag_activation_value` (i.e. `flag_value`) for a non-boolean flag, while boolean flags keep `True`. `get_default` first consults `Context.lookup_default` (the default map), then the local default, and only invokes a callable when `_resolve_lazy_default` passed the value through unchanged — so a `flag_value` that happens to be a class or enum is never called.","lines":["src/click/core.py:3112-3124","src/click/core.py:3227-3249","src/click/core.py:3295-3327","src/click/core.py:2550-2581"],"check":"import click
